I am getting lost in the code, but it seems that in `cmd/restic/cmd_check.go`, line 450, we check if errors were found:

```
	if errorsFound {
		if len(salvagePacks) == 0 {
			printer.E("\nThe repository is damaged and must be repaired. Please follow the troubleshooting guide at https://restic.readthedocs.io/en/stable/077_troubleshooting.html .\n\n")
		}
		return summary, errors.Fatal("repository contains errors")
	}
```

`errorsFound` may be set in several places, e.g. line 385, inside `doReadData()`:

```
	doReadData := func(packs map[restic.ID]int64) {
		p := printer.NewCounter("packs")
		p.SetMax(uint64(len(packs)))
		errChan := make(chan error)

		go chkr.ReadPacks(ctx, packs, p, errChan)

		for err := range errChan {
			errorsFound = true
			summary.NumErrors++
			printer.E("%v\n", err)
			if err, ok := err.(*repository.ErrPackData); ok {
				salvagePacks.Insert(err.PackID)
			}
		}
		p.Done()
	}
```

So, AFAICT, if `errChan` is not empty after the call to `chkr.ReadPacks`, we have `errorsFound == true`.

As for `chkr.ReadPacks`, we have it in `internal/checker/checker.go`, from line 450, with an inner function from line 465:

```
		g.Go(func() error {
			bufRd := bufio.NewReaderSize(nil, maxStreamBufferSize)
			dec, err := zstd.NewReader(nil)
			if err != nil {
				panic(dec)
			}
			defer dec.Close()
			for {
				var ps checkTask
				var ok bool

				select {
				case <-ctx.Done():
					return nil
				case ps, ok = <-ch:
					if !ok {
						return nil
					}
				}

				err := repository.CheckPack(ctx, c.repo.(*repository.Repository), ps.id, ps.blobs, ps.size, bufRd, dec)
				p.Add(1)
				if err == nil {
					continue
				}

				select {
				case <-ctx.Done():
					return nil
				case errChan <- err:
				}
			}
		})
```

Basically, if `repository.CheckPack()` returns anything apart from `nil`, we write to `errChan`, which will later be picked up as an error.

As for `repository.CheckPack()`, we find it in `internal/repository/check.go` from line 40:

```
func CheckPack(ctx context.Context, r *Repository, id restic.ID, blobs []restic.Blob, size int64, bufRd *bufio.Reader, dec *zstd.Decoder) error {
	err := checkPackInner(ctx, r, id, blobs, size, bufRd, dec)
	if err != nil {
		if r.cache != nil {
			// ignore error as there's not much we can do here
			_ = r.cache.Forget(backend.Handle{Type: restic.PackFile, Name: id.String()})
		}

		// retry pack verification to detect transient errors
		err2 := checkPackInner(ctx, r, id, blobs, size, bufRd, dec)
		if err2 != nil {
			err = err2
		} else {
			err = fmt.Errorf("check successful on second attempt, original error %w", err)
		}
	}
	return err
}
```

So, it does a retry in the case of an error, but what it does next, then, is to, helpfully, report that it succeeded on the next try, but unfortunately, as follows from the above **any output here is, down the line, interpreted as an error that yields the message**:

```
The repository is damaged and must be repaired. Please follow the troubleshooting guide at https://restic.readthedocs.io/en/stable/077_troubleshooting.html .
```

It would seem that the veracity of that message cannot be guaranteed, unless somewhere, likely to be in several places, checks are done to revert `errorsFound` to `false` (iff waranted) which, again AFAICT, is not done at present.

On a personal note, I am moving cloud storage providers, and will incur a $100+ annual renewal at the old provider in 3 days if I don't cancel before then, but I am unsure of whether the repository data that I transferred to my new provider is in a sound state, and don't know whether to trust restic's report that it is not...
